WEBVTT

00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:48.510
Do you, Boxy, take this air conditioner

00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:50.260
to be your lawfully wedded...

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:53.220
You can't go through with it,
Boxy! For she is...

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:54.430
your sister!

00:00:55.859 --> 00:00:57.440
And my sister!

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:00.360
And my wife!

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:04.360
And my air conditioner!

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:12.200
Ah, there you are. Behold!

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:18.540
What the name of my ass
is going on here?

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:22.290
The Academy of Inventors'
Annual Competition is approaching.

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:26.720
So I've created the world's
most powerful sewing machine!

00:01:27.645 --> 00:01:29.855
That is an amazing invention!

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:32.340
This isn't the invention, you boob.

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:35.800
Let me just activate it
using my footnological marvel,

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:37.340
the toe-longer.

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:39.220
It's glorious!

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:41.510
That's not the invention either.
Stop guessing.

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:50.230
The theme of the competition is recycling,

00:01:50.230 --> 00:01:53.150
so I'm attempting to recycle
old human parts

00:01:53.150 --> 00:01:56.490
into a new,
environmentally friendly human.

00:01:56.490 --> 00:01:58.660
Hyello, Mr. Professorvich.

00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:01.240
Very extremely good catch for you today.

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:03.870
Luckily was big railroad accident.

00:02:03.870 --> 00:02:06.370
I'll take a pound and a half of hand.

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:15.840
Wow. Is that a flat-lock seam?

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:18.090
Why, thank you for noticing, Amy.

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:21.470
It looks elegant and minimizes blood loss.

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:25.100
Now, the easy part.

00:02:39.070 --> 00:02:40.110
dramatic crescendo

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:44.030
Voilà!

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:47.160
Um, Professor, most people have a head.

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:49.580
Sure, the "in crowd."

00:02:49.580 --> 00:02:51.460
Look, you've come this far.

00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:54.800
You don't wanna lose the contest
over a few style points.

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:56.380
Fine, fine.

00:02:56.380 --> 00:02:58.720
Let's fancy it up with a head.

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:02.050
The head is the part with the teeth
and the sneeze-hole, right?

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:08.980
Any of you presidents need a body?

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:11.060
Can it hit a no-look jump shot?

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:12.440
Uh, sure.

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:13.980
Then why does it need me?

00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:18.900
- Good one, Mommy.
- Whoa!

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:21.280
The gallery of supermodels!

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:23.990
Ugh. Okay, but remember.

00:03:23.990 --> 00:03:25.780
Their eyes are up here.

00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:32.670
Um, do any of you lovely...
lady... people...

00:03:32.670 --> 00:03:34.500
Uh... Oof!

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:37.550
Which of you indistinguishable
human heads wants a body?

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:40.170
Well, is it tall?
I miss doing runway shows

00:03:40.170 --> 00:03:42.260
and intimidating short people.

00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:43.890
The left side's pretty tall.

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:46.010
Trust me, it's attractive.

00:03:46.010 --> 00:03:48.890
It's got thick, luxurious hair
all over the place,

00:03:48.890 --> 00:03:51.600
and several legs that just won't quit.

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:53.900
- And one that will.
- I'll do it.

00:03:55.190 --> 00:03:56.190
Really?

00:03:56.190 --> 00:03:58.860
Of course, dear. It's not the body.

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:00.440
It's how you wear it.

00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:02.910
That's Cara Delevingne!

00:04:02.910 --> 00:04:04.530
She invented eyebrows.

00:04:08.950 --> 00:04:11.830
And... finished!

00:04:11.830 --> 00:04:14.580
Oh no. This won't do.

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:16.000
That's the only head we got.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:17.840
I mean what I'm wearing.

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:19.380
I'm sorry, Cara,

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:22.670
but due to your various
and unusual proportions,

00:04:22.670 --> 00:04:25.140
no clothing will ever fit you.

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:27.390
There's just no possible way to--

00:04:27.390 --> 00:04:28.760
I've got it!

00:04:30.850 --> 00:04:34.650
Simply step into my 3D clothing scanner.

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:40.690
You didn't need that hind leg anyway.

00:04:40.690 --> 00:04:43.530
Now, we'll take your exact measurements...

00:04:44.990 --> 00:04:46.370
Good Lord!

00:04:46.370 --> 00:04:48.490
Encode them in a strand of RNA...

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:53.660
Inject it into
a giant Bolivian silkworm...

00:04:54.670 --> 00:04:57.130
Let it spin its cocoon.

00:04:57.130 --> 00:04:59.130
And six weeks later...

00:04:59.130 --> 00:05:00.420
Presto!

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:03.630
We have a custom-fitted silk garment.

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:10.810
Thanks, but it's a little
Soviet brutalist for me.

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:13.770
Well, that's because
you're looking at the wrong side.

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:15.770
majestic flourish

00:05:15.770 --> 00:05:18.690
This is spectacular.

00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:21.320
I never expected to love something so much

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:23.240
that was excreted from a moth.

00:05:26.950 --> 00:05:29.450
Scientists and scienticians,

00:05:29.450 --> 00:05:33.700
I'm proud to present the finalists
for this year's Inventors' Award.

00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:36.040
First up, Dr. Bubblegum Tate

00:05:36.040 --> 00:05:39.670
and his postdoctoral fellow,
Sweet Clyde Dixon.

00:05:41.460 --> 00:05:45.420
Dr. Dixon and I have devised
a way to generate clean power

00:05:45.420 --> 00:05:48.550
from the billions of basketballs
discarded each year

00:05:48.550 --> 00:05:50.050
by the Big Twelve.

00:05:55.270 --> 00:05:57.100
Ah!

00:05:59.860 --> 00:06:01.820
Next up, last year's winner,

00:06:01.820 --> 00:06:03.820
Professor Ogden Wernstrom.

00:06:03.820 --> 00:06:05.940
Wernstrom!

00:06:06.950 --> 00:06:08.820
Discarded doomsday devices

00:06:08.820 --> 00:06:11.280
pose a grave environmental risk.

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:14.370
So I've invented a way
of reprogramming them

00:06:14.370 --> 00:06:17.790
into lovable companions for the elderly.

00:06:21.340 --> 00:06:23.960
Aw!

00:06:23.960 --> 00:06:25.760
Come along, Explodey.

00:06:28.090 --> 00:06:29.180
And last up...

00:06:31.010 --> 00:06:33.970
Professor Hubert J. Farnsworth.

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:37.940
Colleagues, you witness history tonight.

00:06:37.940 --> 00:06:40.730
The technology to recycle the dead

00:06:40.730 --> 00:06:43.400
back to semi-normal life!

00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:53.780
It's a scientific leap for humanity!

00:06:53.780 --> 00:06:54.790
Questions?

00:06:54.790 --> 00:06:57.700
Who made that dress? It's fabulous!

00:06:57.700 --> 00:06:59.580
Did you not hear what I said?

00:06:59.580 --> 00:07:01.580
I brought a human back to life!

00:07:01.580 --> 00:07:05.000
But that gown brings
sexy back to science!

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:08.090
Are there any questions
about my invention

00:07:08.090 --> 00:07:09.720
and not the damn dress?

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:13.260
And now, to announce the winner.

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:17.220
In the spirit of recycling,
we're reusing last year's envelope.

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:21.400
And the winner was...

00:07:21.400 --> 00:07:23.480
Ogden Wernstrom!

00:07:33.620 --> 00:07:36.540
Cara, over here. Show us the dress!

00:07:36.540 --> 00:07:38.500
Oh, what's going on?

00:07:38.500 --> 00:07:41.000
The photon pressure is knocking me over.

00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:43.750
Who are you wearing?
You look incredible!

00:07:43.750 --> 00:07:45.500
It's a brand-new designer.

00:07:45.500 --> 00:07:46.960
The Professor.

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:48.170
Uh-whuh...

00:07:53.050 --> 00:07:56.140
All my life,
I've pursued my first love,

00:07:56.140 --> 00:07:57.350
science.

00:07:57.350 --> 00:08:00.020
But my career has come to naught.

00:08:00.020 --> 00:08:03.980
It's time to admit I should've
pursued my first love.

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:06.060
Fashion.

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:11.070
Oh, my!

00:08:11.070 --> 00:08:13.070
That'll look better on me.

00:08:23.580 --> 00:08:25.630
I've got a midlife crisis ponytail

00:08:25.630 --> 00:08:27.420
for a Hubert Farnsworth?

00:08:27.420 --> 00:08:29.840
It's pronounced "Hugh-bear."

00:08:29.840 --> 00:08:30.880
It is?

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:32.840
Woo!

00:08:32.840 --> 00:08:36.550
Gorgeous! Like a desiccated Zac Posen.

00:08:36.550 --> 00:08:39.810
Hugh-bear, I'm going to make you a star.

00:08:39.810 --> 00:08:43.140
I've made a few calls.
You're on a magazine cover, baby!

00:08:44.190 --> 00:08:45.270
Oh my!

00:08:45.270 --> 00:08:47.480
I'm on the cover of Vague!

00:08:47.480 --> 00:08:50.860
My mother would be so confused.

00:08:52.530 --> 00:08:54.030
It's Gladys Lennox,

00:08:54.030 --> 00:08:55.990
the world-famous paramecium!

00:08:55.990 --> 00:08:58.620
Star of Mitosis, Lies, and Videotape!

00:08:58.620 --> 00:09:02.200
Is there something we can
help you with, Ms. Lennox?

00:09:02.200 --> 00:09:04.080
I saw the dress you made,

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:05.870
and I must have one.

00:09:05.870 --> 00:09:08.500
I just love the stitch work.

00:09:08.500 --> 00:09:10.960
Thank you. It's holding my neck on.

00:09:10.960 --> 00:09:12.670
Cara's one of a kind,

00:09:12.670 --> 00:09:16.590
not like those mass-produced
Frankensteins you see nowadays.

00:09:16.590 --> 00:09:19.050
Being boneless, clothing never fits me.

00:09:19.050 --> 00:09:21.560
It's like trying to put pants
on a waterbed.

00:09:21.560 --> 00:09:23.850
I once got the pants off a waterbed,

00:09:23.850 --> 00:09:25.520
but that's a very long,

00:09:25.520 --> 00:09:28.650
very dirty story.

00:09:28.650 --> 00:09:31.070
I can make you an outfit, Ms. Lennox,

00:09:31.070 --> 00:09:34.240
but it's a slow, meticulous process.

00:09:34.240 --> 00:09:35.820
Also expensive.

00:09:35.820 --> 00:09:37.110
Money's no object.

00:09:37.110 --> 00:09:39.530
I have contractile vacuoles full of it.

00:09:42.490 --> 00:09:44.710
Gol'durned cytoplasm.

00:09:44.710 --> 00:09:46.120
Yeah, that's it.

00:09:46.120 --> 00:09:49.840
Make sweet asexual love to the scanner.

00:09:49.840 --> 00:09:51.960
No, don't shake your moneymaker!

00:09:51.960 --> 00:09:55.010
Give me less of that!
Don't work it, baby!

00:09:58.470 --> 00:10:00.800
Ooh, I want to give myself an award

00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:02.350
for looking at that dress.

00:10:02.350 --> 00:10:04.430
Who are you wearing, Gladys?

00:10:04.430 --> 00:10:05.890
Oh, what, this little thing?

00:10:05.890 --> 00:10:07.640
It's from the House of Professor.

00:10:07.640 --> 00:10:10.810
Get me the Professor!

00:10:10.810 --> 00:10:12.480
Get me The Professor!

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:14.230
the Professor!

00:10:17.320 --> 00:10:19.820
Tim Gunn here at Milan Fashion Week.

00:10:19.820 --> 00:10:22.580
Today, the hot new old designer

00:10:22.580 --> 00:10:25.620
whose name is on everyone's
silicone-filled lips,

00:10:25.620 --> 00:10:27.620
Hugh-bear Farnts-virt.

00:10:30.130 --> 00:10:31.960
Please, no photons.

00:10:40.140 --> 00:10:41.220
Two minutes!

00:10:41.220 --> 00:10:43.010
I-I-I'm so nervous!

00:10:43.010 --> 00:10:46.560
And I can barely move in
these high-heeled slippers.

00:10:46.560 --> 00:10:48.980
How am I supposed to walk Marcelle?

00:10:50.600 --> 00:10:54.020
Not to worry.
Zoidberg will walk your leggy snake.

00:11:00.780 --> 00:11:03.620
Welcome to the fashion event
of the millennium.

00:11:03.620 --> 00:11:07.580
We call it Haute Cocoon, by The Professor.

00:11:07.580 --> 00:11:08.870
Enjoy.

00:11:21.180 --> 00:11:24.680
Shock me back to life
with a defibrillator

00:11:24.680 --> 00:11:26.890
because I am dead.

00:11:28.930 --> 00:11:31.440
I'm Zoidberg. I'm on the list.

00:11:32.770 --> 00:11:34.360
Sorry, we're full.

00:11:34.360 --> 00:11:36.070
The anteater can come in though.

00:11:37.530 --> 00:11:40.150
But I'm on the list! Right there!

00:11:40.740 --> 00:11:42.240
Now you're not.

00:11:42.240 --> 00:11:46.200
You're dressed like some sort of underpaid
lobster doctor from outer space.

00:11:46.200 --> 00:11:49.040
But I can't afford nicer clothings!

00:11:49.040 --> 00:11:50.710
Then beat it.

00:11:53.080 --> 00:11:56.250
I've never felt so worthless.

00:11:56.250 --> 00:11:58.760
I'll just throw myself in the garbage.

00:12:01.680 --> 00:12:04.430
You can't come in our dumpster
what looking like that.

00:12:04.430 --> 00:12:06.430
Not during Fashion Week.

00:12:18.907 --> 00:12:20.990
You're a hit, Professor!

00:12:20.990 --> 00:12:23.910
You'll be invited to every party,
every soirée,

00:12:23.910 --> 00:12:27.160
every orgy.
- And I'll stay home napping.

00:12:27.160 --> 00:12:28.990
Who's gonna stop me?

00:12:30.450 --> 00:12:32.370
Zoidberg! What are you doing?

00:12:32.370 --> 00:12:33.370
Jumping!

00:12:34.420 --> 00:12:36.080
No, stop.

00:12:36.080 --> 00:12:38.250
You don't get it! Your fabulous clothing

00:12:38.250 --> 00:12:41.970
just makes the other 99% of us
sad and jealous.

00:12:43.930 --> 00:12:47.720
I'm sick of dressing
in factory reject chef's jackets

00:12:47.720 --> 00:12:50.020
and children's flip-flops!

00:12:50.020 --> 00:12:52.230
Goodbye, fancy friends.

00:12:53.850 --> 00:12:55.270
Noooooo!

00:12:55.850 --> 00:12:57.270
Don't do it.

00:13:01.110 --> 00:13:03.860
Zoidberg! Don't kill yourself!

00:13:03.860 --> 00:13:06.780
What? No! I'm just going underwater,

00:13:06.780 --> 00:13:08.780
where the fashion standards are lower.

00:13:11.330 --> 00:13:14.370
Excuse me,
the fashion standards aren't lower.

00:13:14.370 --> 00:13:17.290
Why, look at the flat-lock seam
on my clam shells.

00:13:28.100 --> 00:13:30.470
Professor, when you started your line,

00:13:30.470 --> 00:13:33.100
you were proud the clothes
could fit anyone.

00:13:33.100 --> 00:13:35.890
But it's only the wealthy
who can enjoy them.

00:13:35.890 --> 00:13:37.770
I feel for you, Zoidberg,

00:13:37.770 --> 00:13:41.730
but there's simply no way
to mass-produce my cocoon patterns.

00:13:41.730 --> 00:13:43.860
And moth chow isn't free.

00:13:46.530 --> 00:13:48.910
But, isn't the difference
between couture

00:13:48.910 --> 00:13:51.540
and ready-to-wear
just a matter of technology?

00:13:51.540 --> 00:13:53.870
Uh, well, I suppose it is.

00:13:53.870 --> 00:13:56.120
You know, I've never told anyone this,

00:13:56.120 --> 00:13:58.420
but it's been my lifelong dream

00:13:58.420 --> 00:14:00.590
to be a famous scientist.

00:14:00.590 --> 00:14:02.500
Perhaps I could use science

00:14:02.500 --> 00:14:04.720
to bring fashion to the masses.

00:14:04.720 --> 00:14:07.380
But no, I'm afraid it's completely...

00:14:07.380 --> 00:14:09.090
Eureka!

00:14:09.090 --> 00:14:12.600
Bender, hand me that hat
from my new October line.

00:14:17.900 --> 00:14:21.320
Fly! Fly, my pretties!

00:14:27.320 --> 00:14:29.200
Welcome to Good Morning Tonight.

00:14:29.200 --> 00:14:31.700
Or as I like to say, good mornight.

00:14:33.080 --> 00:14:35.290
I will destroy you
and your entire species

00:14:35.290 --> 00:14:37.410
if you continue to combine those words!

00:14:37.410 --> 00:14:41.290
This mornight, we have a very
special guest in the studio.

00:14:41.290 --> 00:14:44.880
Legendary fashion designer,
Hugh-bear Farntsvirt.

00:14:49.180 --> 00:14:51.090
Morbo has heard through his earpiece

00:14:51.090 --> 00:14:54.260
that this wrinkly human
now offers a clothing line

00:14:54.260 --> 00:14:55.810
for a broader audience.

00:14:55.810 --> 00:14:57.270
Indeed, I do.

00:14:57.270 --> 00:14:59.270
You see, I've created a variant

00:14:59.270 --> 00:15:02.650
on the giant moth I use
for my couture line.

00:15:04.820 --> 00:15:06.570
For a low monthly price,

00:15:06.570 --> 00:15:08.820
you get a hangar moth for your closet.

00:15:08.820 --> 00:15:13.080
And it extrudes a bespoke
tailored outfit every day.

00:15:13.080 --> 00:15:16.910
They're essentially
high-speed 3D silk printers.

00:15:16.910 --> 00:15:19.000
Morbo demands a demonstration!

00:15:26.840 --> 00:15:28.090
This just in.

00:15:28.090 --> 00:15:29.760
I look fabulous!

00:15:29.760 --> 00:15:33.430
These slacks really flatter
Morbo's pulsating buttocks.

00:15:33.430 --> 00:15:34.680
How do I wash them?!

00:15:34.680 --> 00:15:36.810
Oh, no need to wash anything.

00:15:36.810 --> 00:15:39.770
The outfits fall apart
after one day's use,

00:15:39.770 --> 00:15:42.650
but they'd be out of fashion
by then anyway.

00:15:42.650 --> 00:15:44.570
Each night, just dispose of them

00:15:44.570 --> 00:15:46.780
in my patented fash-can.

00:15:51.660 --> 00:15:54.620
It's fast-fashion at its fastiest!

00:15:54.620 --> 00:15:56.370
Where do the clothes go?

00:15:56.370 --> 00:15:58.950
Who knows? Who cares?

00:15:58.950 --> 00:16:00.540
Tell us, Professor Farntsvirt,

00:16:00.540 --> 00:16:02.830
how can our viewers get
your new clothing line?

00:16:02.830 --> 00:16:06.800
Just leave the window open
and the light on.

00:16:06.800 --> 00:16:10.760
Starships were meant to fly

00:16:10.760 --> 00:16:14.930
Hands up and touch the sky

00:16:14.930 --> 00:16:19.060
Can't stop 'cause we're so high

00:16:19.060 --> 00:16:21.190
Let's do this one more time

00:16:22.600 --> 00:16:26.440
Starships were meant to fly

00:16:26.440 --> 00:16:29.990
Hands up and touch the sky

00:16:29.990 --> 00:16:33.030
Let's do this one last time

00:16:34.110 --> 00:16:37.240
Can't stop, we're higher than

00:16:40.200 --> 00:16:41.710
music fades out

00:16:41.710 --> 00:16:45.210
Great look, Zoidberg. Very today.

00:16:45.210 --> 00:16:46.920
Thank you.
Though to be honest,

00:16:46.920 --> 00:16:49.000
I liked yesterday's even better.

00:16:49.000 --> 00:16:51.166
Would it be possible
to get those pants back?

00:16:51.322 --> 00:16:51.920
No!

00:16:51.920 --> 00:16:54.510
They made my hips look like I had hips,

00:16:54.510 --> 00:16:56.430
but I threw them in the fash-can.

00:16:56.430 --> 00:16:59.890
Forget it. Yesterday's pants
are completely out of date.

00:16:59.890 --> 00:17:02.940
Hugh-bear, Zoidberg knows what he likes,

00:17:02.940 --> 00:17:05.940
and that means he has something
even better than fashion.

00:17:05.940 --> 00:17:07.650
He has style.

00:17:07.650 --> 00:17:08.900
Uh-whuh?

00:17:08.900 --> 00:17:10.780
Plus, isn't it more environmental

00:17:10.780 --> 00:17:13.860
to wear a piece of clothing twice
before you throw it away?

00:17:15.273 --> 00:17:19.305
I would gladly crawl in after them,
but I can't fit in the fash-can.

00:17:19.330 --> 00:17:20.660
Anything can fit!

00:17:20.660 --> 00:17:25.370
The fash-can is a multi-dimensional
wormhole through space and time

00:17:25.370 --> 00:17:27.460
and probably some other things.

00:17:27.460 --> 00:17:29.340
Allow me to demonstrate.

00:17:29.340 --> 00:17:31.760
Jessica. Oh, Jessica!

00:17:34.470 --> 00:17:36.800
Go on, girl. Get your peanut.

00:17:43.730 --> 00:17:47.270
- Professor! That's horrible!
- Relax.

00:17:47.270 --> 00:17:50.400
I get a new pet every day
to go with my outfits.

00:17:50.400 --> 00:17:52.230
That's slightly worse!

00:17:52.230 --> 00:17:54.820
It's simply too dangerous
to pass through

00:17:54.820 --> 00:17:56.660
a trillion-light-year wormhole

00:17:56.660 --> 00:17:58.320
for a pair of pants.

00:17:58.320 --> 00:18:01.450
Why, the temporal distortion alone
could render you...

00:18:07.500 --> 00:18:09.000
Whoa!

00:18:09.000 --> 00:18:10.750
That's what I was gonna say.

00:18:10.750 --> 00:18:12.250
We're coming out of the wormhole.

00:18:12.250 --> 00:18:14.340
There's a planet ahead!

00:18:22.310 --> 00:18:24.100
Shmoly moly!

00:18:24.100 --> 00:18:26.350
If any civilization was here,

00:18:26.350 --> 00:18:29.100
it's been smothered
by the Professor's clothes.

00:18:29.100 --> 00:18:31.440
The devastation is too much.

00:18:31.440 --> 00:18:33.440
It's beyond comprehension.

00:18:33.440 --> 00:18:34.440
dramatic crescendo

00:18:34.440 --> 00:18:35.490
My pants!

00:18:42.990 --> 00:18:46.210
Alright. Let's snag Zoidberg's pants
and get outta here.

00:18:46.210 --> 00:18:48.330
Bender, deploy the space hooker.

00:18:48.330 --> 00:18:50.040
Deploy her? I barely know her!

00:18:51.500 --> 00:18:53.500
Ah, just kidding.
I'm a regular customer.

00:19:01.050 --> 00:19:02.970
It's a total shirt storm!

00:19:02.970 --> 00:19:04.520
What do we do? Panic?

00:19:07.730 --> 00:19:10.650
A flock of Canadian bras
got sucked into the engine!

00:19:10.650 --> 00:19:12.150
We'll have to crash-land!

00:19:16.990 --> 00:19:18.490
And we're fine.

00:19:18.490 --> 00:19:21.240
I believe we were spared for one reason,

00:19:21.240 --> 00:19:22.700
and one reason only.

00:19:22.700 --> 00:19:25.620
To save Zoidberg's pants.
Come on!

00:19:28.040 --> 00:19:29.040
Wuzzat?

00:19:29.040 --> 00:19:30.960
Moths! Huge ones!

00:19:30.960 --> 00:19:34.460
They must be feasting
on high-protein sportswear.

00:19:34.460 --> 00:19:37.380
Actually, it's not the moths
that eat clothes.

00:19:37.380 --> 00:19:38.800
It's the caterpillars.

00:19:38.800 --> 00:19:41.720
People don't know that
because they're tiny and hard to see.

00:19:59.036 --> 00:20:00.067
And we're fine.

00:20:01.200 --> 00:20:03.660
What's this metal thing
we're cowering behind?

00:20:03.660 --> 00:20:05.990
Some sort of abandoned structure.

00:20:05.990 --> 00:20:09.160
The Professor really did
destroy a civilization!

00:20:09.160 --> 00:20:12.710
It's history's most
brutal crime of fashion.

00:20:12.710 --> 00:20:14.460
Ah, my pants!

00:20:14.460 --> 00:20:16.590
There, in that pile of crisp whites!

00:20:17.130 --> 00:20:20.300
Oh, to wear them just once more.

00:20:22.800 --> 00:20:24.970
The Professor's garbage pets!

00:20:24.970 --> 00:20:27.180
- Run for your lives!
- No!

00:20:27.180 --> 00:20:29.390
I am a professional fashion model.

00:20:29.390 --> 00:20:31.850
We do not run. We strut.

00:20:31.850 --> 00:20:35.440
And we do not leave our comrades'
fallen pants behind.

00:20:35.440 --> 00:20:37.320
Sounds good!
We'll meet you in the ship.

00:20:37.320 --> 00:20:39.110
And by meet you, I mean ditch you.

00:20:45.320 --> 00:20:47.030
Cara, look out!

00:20:53.580 --> 00:20:55.130
Ah! Hiya!

00:20:56.670 --> 00:20:58.500
electric guitar riff

00:20:59.460 --> 00:21:01.970
Everybody on board
before we get buried!

00:21:07.470 --> 00:21:09.520
Jump!

00:21:09.520 --> 00:21:10.970
Ah!

00:21:10.970 --> 00:21:13.060
Hang on! You'll be okay!

00:21:13.060 --> 00:21:15.350
But my grip is crumpling the pants!

00:21:15.350 --> 00:21:16.730
So I'll iron them!

00:21:16.730 --> 00:21:18.980
No! The material's too cheap!

00:21:18.980 --> 00:21:21.530
It won't stand up to
even the lowest setting.

00:21:21.530 --> 00:21:23.860
I'll wear them wrinkled!
I can pull it off!

00:21:23.860 --> 00:21:26.660
Don't be a fool!
What is one life

00:21:26.660 --> 00:21:30.120
against a completely decent pair of pants?

00:21:30.120 --> 00:21:31.870
Goodbye.

00:21:32.370 --> 00:21:35.870
Cara, no!

00:21:49.300 --> 00:21:51.010
Oh, good! You're back!

00:21:51.010 --> 00:21:54.230
I can toss yesterday's
conquistador ensemble

00:21:54.230 --> 00:21:55.850
without damaging the ship.

00:21:59.820 --> 00:22:02.730
Stop! We saw where
the clothes are going!

00:22:02.730 --> 00:22:05.030
You've wiped out an entire planet!

00:22:05.030 --> 00:22:06.740
Wiped out, you say?

00:22:06.740 --> 00:22:08.660
Well, if everyone is already dead,

00:22:08.660 --> 00:22:10.280
I don't see the problem.

00:22:10.280 --> 00:22:11.990
Someone might still be alive

00:22:11.990 --> 00:22:14.160
under all those vegan leather pants!

00:22:14.160 --> 00:22:17.170
Those are not breathable!
You gotta do something!

00:22:18.091 --> 00:22:19.500
Very well.

00:22:20.500 --> 00:22:23.300
The ship's log should hold
the space-time coordinates

00:22:23.300 --> 00:22:25.090
of the planet you came from.

00:22:25.090 --> 00:22:28.180
I'll send them a jaws of life
or something.

00:22:28.180 --> 00:22:29.720
Aah!

00:22:29.720 --> 00:22:31.760
What is it? Something surprising?

00:22:31.760 --> 00:22:35.310
The planet you went to was... was...

00:22:35.310 --> 00:22:36.310
Earth!

00:22:37.980 --> 00:22:39.560
But that's our planet, right?

00:22:39.560 --> 00:22:41.900
And we're not buried
under miles of laundry.

00:22:41.900 --> 00:22:43.980
Don't you see, you imbecile?

00:22:43.980 --> 00:22:45.820
No, sir, I'm afraid I don't.

00:22:45.820 --> 00:22:48.610
It's Earth in the future!

00:22:48.610 --> 00:22:52.580
We sent the clothes... to our own future!

00:22:59.500 --> 00:23:03.300
You finally did it,
you fast-fashionistas!

00:23:03.300 --> 00:23:06.420
Damn you all to TJ Maxx!

00:23:06.420 --> 00:23:09.800
What kind of world
have we left for our children?

00:23:09.800 --> 00:23:11.260
Or their children?

00:23:11.260 --> 00:23:13.390
Or their children's children? Wait.

00:23:13.390 --> 00:23:16.140
How into the future
did we send the clothes?

00:23:16.140 --> 00:23:17.560
What time is it?